	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163378">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Chemistry courses involve studying the composition, properties, and reactions of substances. These courses typically explore such concepts as the behaviors of solids, liquids, and gases; acid/base and oxidation/reduction reactions; and atomic structure. Chemical formulas and equations and nuclear reactions are also studied.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163378</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Chemistry</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Chemistry courses involve studying the composition, properties, and reactions of substances. These courses typically explore such concepts as the behaviors of solids, liquids, and gases; acid/base and oxidation/reduction reactions; and atomic structure. Chemical formulas and equations and nuclear reactions are also studied.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03101</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>Chemistry</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163379">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Usually taken after a comprehensive initial study of chemistry, Chemistry�Advanced Studies courses cover chemical properties and interactions in more detail. Advanced chemistry topics include organic chemistry, thermodynamics, electrochemistry, macromolecules, kinetic theory, and nuclear chemistry.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163379</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Chemistry�Advanced Studies</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Usually taken after a comprehensive initial study of chemistry, Chemistry�Advanced Studies courses cover chemical properties and interactions in more detail. Advanced chemistry topics include organic chemistry, thermodynamics, electrochemistry, macromolecules, kinetic theory, and nuclear chemistry.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03102</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>Chemistry�Advanced Studies</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163380">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Organic Chemistry courses involve the study of organic molecules and functional groups. Topics covered may include nomenclature, bonding molecular structure and reactivity, reaction mechanisms, and current spectroscopic techniques.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163380</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Organic Chemistry</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Organic Chemistry courses involve the study of organic molecules and functional groups. Topics covered may include nomenclature, bonding molecular structure and reactivity, reaction mechanisms, and current spectroscopic techniques.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03103</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>Organic Chemistry</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163381">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Usually taken after completing a calculus course, Physical Chemistry courses cover chemical kinetics, quantum mechanics, molecular structure, molecular spectroscopy, and statistical mechanics.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163381</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Physical Chemistry</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Usually taken after completing a calculus course, Physical Chemistry courses cover chemical kinetics, quantum mechanics, molecular structure, molecular spectroscopy, and statistical mechanics.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03104</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>Physical Chemistry</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163382">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Conceptual Chemistry courses are practical, nonquantitative chemistry courses designed for students who desire an understanding of chemical concepts and applications.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163382</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Conceptual Chemistry</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Conceptual Chemistry courses are practical, nonquantitative chemistry courses designed for students who desire an understanding of chemical concepts and applications.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03105</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>Conceptual Chemistry</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163383">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Following the curricula recommended by the College Board, AP Chemistry courses usually follow high school chemistry and second-year algebra. Concepts covered may include the structure of matter; bonding of intermolecular forces; chemical reactions; kinetics; thermodynamics; and chemical equilibrium. For each concept, these courses emphasize the development of scientific inquiry and reasoning skills, such as designing a plan for collecting data, analyzing data, applying mathematical routines, and connecting concepts in and across domains. AP Chemistry courses include college-level laboratory investigations.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163383</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>AP Chemistry</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Following the curricula recommended by the College Board, AP Chemistry courses usually follow high school chemistry and second-year algebra. Concepts covered may include the structure of matter; bonding of intermolecular forces; chemical reactions; kinetics; thermodynamics; and chemical equilibrium. For each concept, these courses emphasize the development of scientific inquiry and reasoning skills, such as designing a plan for collecting data, analyzing data, applying mathematical routines, and connecting concepts in and across domains. AP Chemistry courses include college-level laboratory investigations.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03106</skos:notation>
		<skos:prefLabel>AP Chemistry</skos:prefLabel>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/NI001517163386">
		<dc:creator>Common Education Data Standards</dc:creator>
		<dc:description>Chemistry�Independent Study courses, often conducted with instructors as mentors, enable students to explore scientific topics of interest, using advanced methods of scientific inquiry and experimentation. These courses may be offered in conjunction with other rigorous science courses or may serve as an opportunity to explore a topic of special interest.</dc:description>
		<dc:identifier rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#token">NI001517163386</dc:identifier>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept" />
		<rdfs:label>Chemistry�Independent Study</rdfs:label>
		<skos:definition>Chemistry�Independent Study courses, often conducted with instructors as mentors, enable students to explore scientific topics of interest, using advanced methods of scientific inquiry and experimentation. These courses may be offered in conjunction with other rigorous science courses or may serve as an opportunity to explore a topic of special interest.</skos:definition>
		<skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/CEDStandards/terms/C001517" />
		<skos:notation>03147</skos:notation>
